WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.850
Qadamar decides, on every request, who may see or do what.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:07.700
And it keeps a record of every decision.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.273
Think of each request as a marble. It passes a row

00:00:11.273 --> 00:00:13.947
of gates, always in the same order. A single no is

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:16.620
enough to stop it.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.190
First case: a payment. Whoever created it can neither approve it

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.860
nor release it. A second person has to.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.055
In an emergency, you can break the glass. Access lasts four

00:00:31.055 --> 00:00:36.350
hours at most, never for yourself, an alert goes out, and

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.645
someone else reviews it afterwards. But it never grants a clearance

00:00:41.645 --> 00:00:46.940
level you don't already hold.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.620
Documents carry a level, from L zero to L three. You

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.400
cannot read above your level. And you cannot write below it,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.180
so a secret never leaks downwards.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.913
Context is checked on every request: network, time, device, recent sign-in.

00:01:03.913 --> 00:01:07.747
Off the trusted network, the reachable level drops. And if a

00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:11.580
signal is missing, the answer is no.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.540
To change a rule, you first see its impact: who gains

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.600
access, and who loses it. Then a second person approves. Nobody

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.660
can approve their own change.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.410
Every decision is written to a chained log. If one entry

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.260
is altered, the chain breaks, and verification shows it.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.070
Your access rules in one place, instead of scattered through the

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:36.980
code. Qadamar.
